post_install() {
    cat <<'EOF'
rgy: READ THIS. Despite the name, searchctl is NOT a systemd service, is
NOT part of systemd, and does NOT require systemd. It is a plain CLI: run
it, it walks the tree you give --root, it exits. The searchd daemon is a
separate, OPTIONAL binary. rgy-autostart uses a systemd USER unit on Linux
only as one convenient way to start that daemon at login; nothing in rgy is
installed into, or depends on, the system init. rgy works with no systemd,
with systemd disabled, or with no init at all.

  ============================================================
  Do you want to install and configure the rgy search daemon NOW?
  ============================================================

  This package installs files as root and CANNOT register a daemon in
  YOUR user session (a systemd user unit, a launchd agent, a login item).
  Only you can do that, in your own terminal, as your own user:

    YES -- enable the daemon (recommended on a desktop):
        rgy-autostart install
      This writes a systemd USER unit (rgy-searchd.service) and arms it with
      `enable --now`: the daemon starts now and at every login, with a live
      inotify change feed. No root, no elevation, no dialog.
      Check:  rgy-autostart doctor
      Undo:   rgy-autostart uninstall
      The one-line installer can also do this after the fact, with no
      download:  install.sh --activate

    NO -- keep it daemon-free. Then be precise about what needs what:

      * searchctl needs an INDEX, not a daemon. Index at least once:
            searchctl --root ~ --reindex --count '*'
        It walks the tree, builds the snapshot and saves it. Without a
        daemon a later plain query reuses that snapshot for 30 minutes
        (--max-age), then rebuilds on demand; --max-age 0 rebuilds every
        query. Schedule the command daily with a systemd user timer
        (see the README) to refresh it without any resident process.

      * rgxy needs a RUNNING searchd, not an index. Indexing once does NOT
        make rgxy work: with no daemon it says "no connection to the daemon".
        To TRY it with nothing installed or configured, start searchd in the
        foreground on a temporary endpoint and point rgxy at it:
            searchd --root ~ --listen uds:/tmp/rgy-try.sock &
            rgxy --endpoint uds:/tmp/rgy-try.sock --query invoice
            rgxy --endpoint uds:/tmp/rgy-try.sock --tui
        Stop it with Ctrl-C when done. No unit, no login entry, nothing
        registered. For one question with no daemon at all, use searchctl:
            searchctl --root ~ "invoice"

  Do NOT run the daemon and a scheduled rebuild on the same tree: both
  write the same index file, and a scheduled --reindex would overwrite
  the daemon's live state.
EOF
}

post_upgrade() {
    post_install
}
